<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-misconceptions-about-critical-points" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Misconceptions about critical points
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m> f \colon X \to \R </m> be a real-valued function defined on an interval <m> X \subseteq \R </m>. <alert> The following statements are false! </alert> Explain why the statement is incorrect, and disprove them by providing a counterexample.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m> f </m> attains a local extremum at a point <m> c \in X </m>, then <m> c </m> is a critical point of <m> f </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        If <m> I </m> contains an endpoint, then it is possible for <m> f </m> to attain a local extremum at such a point without having a critical point there. For example, let <m> X = \clint{ 0 }{ 1 } </m> and <m> f \of{ x } = x </m>. Then <m> f </m> has local extrema at <m> 0 </m> and <m> 1 </m>, but <m> f </m> has no critical points.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If a point <m> c \in X </m> is a critical point of <m> f </m>, then <m> f </m> attains a local extremum at <m> c </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        Not every critical point is a local extremum. For example, let <m> X = \R </m> and <m> f \of{ x } = \sqrt[ 3 ]{ x } </m>. Then <m> f </m> has a critical point at <m> 0 </m>, but <m> f </m> does not attain a local extremum at <m> 0 </m>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If a point <m> c \in X </m> is a regular point of <m> f </m>, then <m> c </m> has a neighborhood in <m> X </m> on which <m> f </m> is monotone.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        If <m> f </m> is not continuously differentiable, then <m> f' \of{ x } </m> might be negative for points arbitrarily close to <m> x </m>. For example, let <m> X = \R </m> and <m> f \of{ x } = x^{ 2 } \sin \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ x } } + \frac{ x }{ 2 } </m>. <m> f </m> has a regular point at <m> 0 </m>, but is not locally monotone near <m> 0 </m>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m> f </m> is strictly monotone if and only if <m> f </m> has no critical points.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        If <m> f </m> has no critical points, then it is strictly monotone, but the converse implication does not hold. For example, let <m> X = \R </m> and <m> f \of{ x } = x^{ 3 } </m>. Then <m> 0 </m> is a critical point of <m> f </m>, but <m> f </m> is strictly monotone.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
</exercise>
